== Trivia ==
* Besides the music listed here, Ridiculon have released three unofficial albums; ''[https://ridiculon.bandcamp.com/album/the-binding-of-isaac-lullabies The Binding of Isaac - Lullabies]'' (which has a colon instead of a hyphen on Spotify), ''[https://ridiculon.bandcamp.com/album/the-binding-of-isaac-mutations The Binding of Isaac: Mutations]'', and ''[https://ridiculon.bandcamp.com/album/shards-of-repentance Shards of Repentance]''.
* Ridiculon have also released a single titled ''[https://ridiculon.bandcamp.com/album/the-binding-of-christmas The Binding of Christmas]'', though this is only available on Bandcamp.
* Devoid and The Fallen Angel's descriptions are simply a line of underscores on Bandcamp, while they have real descriptions in the Spotify release. This was likely done to prevent spoilers, as {{s|Dark Room}} was new to Rebirth and not present in Flash Isaac. Terminus also has these underscores, but the description is entirely absent in the Spotify release.
* The Legend of Bum-Bo, a Binding of Isaac spinoff, has a title theme that sounds similar to and shares a name with Rebirth’s title theme.
** The title motif can also be heard in ''Living in the Light'' and ''Chorus Mortis'', as well as ''Terminal Lucidity'', [[Delirium]]'s theme in Antibirth. 
* In an [https://steamcommunity.com/games/250900/announcements/detail/2909846985626025208 interview on Steam], Ridiculon producer Jon Evans listed the OST songs he is most proud of: “‘Sodden Hollow’ for its dripping groove. ‘Sheol’ for even being allowed to be so terrifying in a game OST. ‘Delirium’ for its dank heaviness.” He also points out that “every track we’ve written for Repentance is our fave.”
* {{s|???}}’s and {{s|Home}}’s music both have “heavy” layers that normally cannot be heard in-game since their respective floors do not contain enemies.
** {{dlc+|r}} ???’s theme can now be heard in-game by various methods, such as using {{i|Red Key}} in ???.{{dlc-}}
** Home's layer theme is not referenced in the <code>music.xml</code> file, and as such, can never be heard in game, even if you were to somehow spawn enemies in Home.
* The filename of Jesus Loves Viggo alludes to Viggo Bossi, Isaac’s voice actor, who sings on the track.
* The filename of the secret room theme, ''secret to everyone'', is a reference to ''The Legend of Zelda'', where secret rooms contain an NPC that says, "It's a secret to everybody."

